  "scriptId": "src-09-square-root-applications-arrangements",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Square-Root Applications and Square Arrangements",
    "description": "Translate rows, collections, payments and age-year stories into square or square-root equations.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use stories to reveal when the same unknown is multiplied by itself: exact square arrangements, leftovers, restoring a damaged square, group-size-squared distributions, paise collections, a herd equation with √x, year-age x², and a perfect-square payment capstone. Every block names what x means before doing algebra and checks the result back in the story.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": "Teach the chapter application patterns where a story hides n² or √n. Cover square arrangements with rows equal to items per row, leftovers after forming the largest square, restoring a damaged square arrangement, distributions where each person receives a multiple of the group size, and collections where each member contributes as many paise as there are members. Include the camel/herd equation containing √x, the nineteenth-century age pattern where a person is x years old in year x², and the payment-per-run pattern where total payment becomes a perfect square. Every story should identify what the unknown represents before forming the equation and should finish with a story-level check.",
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5, Square Roots and Cube Roots. Approved scope: 31"
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn to spot the square hiding inside a story.\n\nRows equal items per row, members paying as many paise as there are members, and payment per run equal to runs—all of them quietly create n².",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A square arrangement has the same number of rows and items in each row.\n\nIf there are n rows and n trees per row, total trees = n².",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "So with 17,956 trees:\n\nn² = 17,956\nn = √17,956 = 134\n\nThe side of a square arrangement is the square root of the total.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
